WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.870
Now let's talk about the principles of

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:03.710
creating skills, when you should create

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.310
them, and how many skills is actually

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.510
too much. Plus also how to revise your

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.390
skills as you go, so that slowly but

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.030
surely you're building up this amazing

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.750
teammate with Cloud Code that will just

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.710
learn how you work and can turbocharge

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.710
your business in practically all of the

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.030
workflows that now can be automated. As

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.470
a general principle, I think if you're

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.790
finding yourself prompting Cloud Code

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.190
twice or more than twice, then you

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.950
should save that as a skill. And the

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.670
reason for this is that it's actually

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.230
really easy to delete a skill, refine a

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.590
skill, or even just forget about it. But

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.790
for a lot of power users, having many

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.710
skills is actually more of an advantage

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.830
rather than a disadvantage in my view.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.350
To give one quick example, one of the

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.150
things that you can definitely do in

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.350
order to align yourself better with your

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.070
agent and to make sure that the output

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.950
that they give you is what you're after,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.710
you can actually add in your prompt this

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.430
simple snippet where if you want to do,

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.910
let's say, a particular task, you can

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.150
have it ask you clarifying questions to

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.630
make sure that you're aligned. And when

00:00:58.640 --> 00:00:59.990
I was personally working with these

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.510
agents a lot, I did find myself adding

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.630
this prompt and typing this prompt more

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.430
than two times per day. And so what I

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.630
did is to just create a skill around it.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.190
And so now, if I have a prompt that I

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.750
want to sort of align with my agent

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.670
with, I just type in {slash} align and

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.910
then give a number here on the number of

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.110
questions that I want it to ask me. And

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.950
apart from that, I actually improve the

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.910
skill some more so that apart from

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.710
asking me questions, whenever it's

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.310
doable, it actually gives me

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.470
multiple-choice answers for the

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.670
question. So that, let's say, if I'm

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.310
just typing the answers here, I can just

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.550
type in the question number, the answer

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.030
to that question with a letter, and just

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.790
go forward from there, which is much

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.630
faster to getting us to a point where we

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.590
are aligned on what we want before I let

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.230
it go off and do some deep work for me.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.510
And if you look at that skill, which I

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.270
will also provide as a resource below,

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.750
it has a description here of asking us

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.750
short number clarifying questions so

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.230
that direction is clear before effort is

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.630
spent. And then there's some guidance

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.950
here on how to use it, some processes

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.430
like the first step of what it should

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.870
do, some guidance to offer short layman

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.710
language just by nature of my

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.870
preference, and so on and so forth. So,

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.950
it's literally just a recipe card that

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.230
whenever this skill is invoked, that is

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.830
what the agent will follow. And the

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:10.910
great thing about these agentic

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.390
platforms is that creating these skills

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.470
is really, really easy. So, you can

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.550
obviously describe the skill yourself in

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.350
that session, and just have Cloud Code

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.870
save the skill for you. But, what I'm

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.910
finding is that when you have a work

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.990
session with Cloud Code, and you think

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.030
that whole conversation can be saved as

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.270
a skill, that is one of the most

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.550
effective plus natural way for you to

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.190
build useful skills for your own work.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.910
An example of how I would do it, let's

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.350
say you have a session where you are

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.910
conducting deep research, and you can

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.630
see here that you already provided specs

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.670
of what it is that you want for this

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.350
research piece, and what it is that you

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.110
do not want whenever this task is done.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.870
What you can do is by the end of the

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.550
session, you can just ask it to make a

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.710
skill, let's say, called deep research

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.030
based on this session, and what I

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.190
usually do with that align skill is that

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.310
I just invoke it so that this skill is

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.509
as good as it can be based on my

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:01.790
preferences. So, if I fire off that

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.270
prompt, then it will ask you some

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.790
clarifying questions on how to build

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.229
this skill, which you can just answer,

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:08.229
after which Cloud Code will build out

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:09.830
this skill for you. So, you can see here

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.229
for me, because I already have this

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:12.710
skill installed, it's flagging that

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.750
exact fact to me that there is a name

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.670
collision. It's also asking about the

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.550
intake style, which styles to keep, and

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.550
so on and so forth. And so, my advice to

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.270
you as you build up your skill library

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.830
is that whenever you work with Cloud

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.430
Code, think about the sessions where you

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.070
feel like that is something that you

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.670
will need to do the next day, or

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.710
sessions where you felt more productive,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.590
to just run this prompt and just have

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.280
Cloud Code create that skill for you.
